      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "We present the case of a post-menopausal female who, following a prolonged period of vomiting and diarrhoea, presented acutely with Wernicke's; encephalopathy, chest pain, ST-segment elevation and congestive cardiac failure associated with hypotension. Coronary angiography demonstrated no abnormality. Haemodynamics improved significantly in the short-term following intravenous thiamine replacement, with complete resolution of all ST-segment abnormalities and normalization of left ventricular function at six-week follow-up. Shoshin beriberi in the context of metabolic alkalosis, secondary to severe electrolyte depletion, is exceptional and is a differential diagnosis to be considered in all malnourished patients presenting with symptoms and signs suggestive of an acute coronary syndrome, especially those with a history of alcohol-dependence."},
